rrives late (after index 1+ of the new block), the `seen` map retains stale entries from the previous block. This could cause valid flashblocks from the new block to be incorrectly deduplicated if they share the same index as a previous block's flashblocks (they would have different `payload_id`, so this is mitigated by the `(payload_id, index)` key). However, the `seen` map will never be cleared in this scenario.\n\n**Recommendation:** Also reset when `payload_id` differs from `current_payload_id` regardless of `index`.\n\n### 3. **[Medium] Inconsistent metrics: `flashblock_multi_source_wins` vs scoped metrics**\n`multi.rs:137-141` vs `multi.rs:197-209`\n\nThe \"wins\" counter is emitted via raw `metrics::counter!` with a dynamic `\"source\"` label, while other metrics use the `#[derive(Metrics)]` with a `flashblock_multi_source` scope. This creates inconsistency:\n- `flashblock_multi_source_deduplicated_total` (scoped)\n- `flashblock_multi_source_wins` (raw, unscoped)\n\nDynamic label cardinality is also a concern — `source` is `state.index.to_string()`, which allocates a `String` on every successful flashblock delivery.\n\n**Recommendation:** Either add the wins counter to the `MultiSourceMetrics` struct or use a consistent naming convention. Cache the source label string in `SourceState` to avoid per-poll allocation.\n\n### 4. **[Medium] Error backoff doesn't actually delay for `stream::Iter` test streams**\n`multi.rs:165-178`\n\nThe backoff creates a `tokio::time::sleep` future, but the tests use synchronous `stream::Iter` iterators. Since `stream::Iter` returns `Poll::Ready` immediately, by the time the backoff is checked on the next `poll_next`, the 500ms sleep hasn't elapsed — but in tests with `#[tokio::test]`, the tokio timer mock might auto-advance. This means `test_per_source_error_backoff` and `test_error_mixed_with_ok` don't truly validate that the backoff delays polling. The tests pass because source B delivers immediately, not because the backoff mechanism works correctly.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add a test using `tokio::time::pause()` + `tokio::time::advance()` to verify that a backed-off source is actually skipped for 500ms.\n\n### 5. **[Low] `any_pending = true` in duplicate branch may cause spurious wakeups**\n`multi.rs:161`\n\nWhen a duplicate is found (occupied entry), `any_pending` is set to `true` and the loop continues. But the loop doesn't re-poll the same source — it moves to the next source. If all remaining sources are done and no other sources are pending, this causes the stream to return `Poll::Pending` even though no waker is registered to wake it again. With synchronous iterators this is fine (they're all `Ready`), but with async sources this could stall the stream.\n\n```rust\nEntry::Occupied(entry) =\u003e {\n    // ...\n    any_pending = true;  // \u003c-- should this continue polling remaining sources instead?\n}\n```\n\nActually, looking more carefully: the loop does continue to the next source, so this is more of a correctness nuance. If all other sources are `done` after a duplicate, the stream returns `Pending` without a waker registered for any live source. This is technically a **waker contract violation** (returning `Pending` without ensuring `cx.waker()` will be called).\n\n**Recommendation:** Only set `any_pending = true` after the duplicate branch if there are other non-done sources that haven't been polled yet, or restructure to continue polling in the same invocation.\n\n### 6. **[Low] `poll_offset` increments unconditionally, even when no meaningful poll occurs**\n`multi.rs:95-96`\n\nThe round-robin offset advances on every `poll_next` call, not just when a result is yielded. Under high-frequency polling (common with tokio), this causes the offset to cycle rapidly. This is harmless for correctness but means fairness isn't truly per-item — it's per-poll-attempt.\n\n### 7. **[Low] `requires` attribute on `flashblock_consensus` may not work with `Vec` args**\n`args.rs:79`\n\n```rust\n#[arg(long, default_value_t = false, requires = \"flashblocks_urls\")]\npub flashblock_consensus: bool,\n```\n\nWith clap, `requires = \"flashblocks_urls\"` on a `Vec\u003cUrl\u003e` field means \"the argument must be present,\" but `Vec` fields are always present (as empty). This means the `requires` constraint is always satisfied — it won't error if `--flashblock-consensus` is passed without any `--flashblocks-url`.\n\n**Recommendation:** Use a custom validator or `conflicts_with`/`requires` on the value level, or add a runtime check.\n\n### 8. **[Cosmetic] Semicolons added in unrelated code**\n`rpc/src/eth/mod.rs:161,202`\n\nThe \"fix\" commit (`afff7887a`) adds trailing semicolons to `return` statements that previously used implicit-return-from-block style. While valid, this is a style-only change mixed into the feature branch.\n\n---\n\n## Positive Observations\n\n- **Generic `Stream` parameter** on `MultiSourceFlashBlockStream\u003cS\u003e` — excellent for testability, follows static dispatch best practices (Ch. 6).\n- **Clean API migration** from `Option\u003cUrl\u003e` to `Vec\u003cUrl\u003e` — backward compatible (empty Vec = no flashblocks).\n- **Good test coverage** — 8 test cases covering passthrough, dedup, cross-validation, errors, termination, block reset, and empty sources.\n- **Appropriate use of `eyre::Result`** for the stream item type, consistent with the existing `WsFlashBlockStream` contract (Ch. 4).\n- **Metrics instrumentation** for observability (dedup count, mismatches, active sources).\n- **Round-robin polling** for fairness across sources.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| # | Severity | Issue | File:Line |\n|---|----------|-------|-----------|\n| 1 | High | Unbounded `seen` map growth if reset heuristic fails | `multi.rs:49` |\n| 2 | Medium | Dedup reset only on `index==0` — fragile ordering | `multi.rs:122` |\n| 3 | Medium | Inconsistent metrics naming + per-poll String alloc | `multi.rs:137` |\n| 4 | Medium | Backoff not truly validat